{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"a2dc1855-72b0-42c4-991f-99b2fafd717c","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\": {\"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\", \"additionalContext\": \"## Today\\nTuesday, September 22, 2026\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Knowledge Base\\n\\n286 compiled articles (214 concepts, 71 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here \\u2014 read it only when a question actually needs it:\\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \\\"<query>\\\" --top-k 5`\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Recent Daily Log\\n\\n**Context:** Ran `/dev-sync` to bring the Mac in line with GitHub, decided two open GPS items, then ran `/closeout` for 2026-09-22.\\n\\n**Key Exchanges:**\\n- Open decision on the 16:28 Mac wake for Perch/VALD pulls \\u2192 Eric chose Option 2 (leave it). He also confirmed the Windows GPS job is stopped.\\n\\n**Decisions Made:**\\n- **No `pmset` wake.** Perch and VALD may land up to 1 hour late when the Mac sleeps. Undo/enable command kept in `DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/STATUS.md`: `sudo pmset repeat wakeorpoweron MTWRFSU 16:28:00`.\\n- Windows GPS writer marked stopped (per Eric's report; not verifiable from the Mac). Two writers = two mismatched databases.\\n- Did **not** run `compile.py` \\u2014 a run costs ~$1.28, so it is Eric's call, not an auto-action.\\n\\n**Lessons Learned:**\\n- Transcript log conflicts between Mac and Windows resolve cleanly with **union merge** \\u2014 keeps every line from both machines, no `.from-*` files to clean up.\\n- A **folder name with a space** (`DEV/Linda Projects`) broke one sync step; had to redo it quoted.\\n- The backup manifest does not auto-discover new repos. `TOS_Evidence_Brief` was missing, which made the 09:05 backup report an error. Adding it to `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json` fixed it.\\n- Tier A memory does not cross machines \\u2014 durable GPS decisions were written into the repo's `docs/STATUS.md` plus `global-memory/MEMORY.md` so Windows sees them.\\n\\n**Sync results (146 repos walked):**\\n- Pulled: `_INBOX` (1), `DataBase_GPS_Reporting` (2). Pushed: `CommandCentre` (`server.out`). Cloned: `TOS_Evidence_Brief` (made on Windows 9/21). 22 active, 117 dormant and clean. No conflicts.\\n\\n**Files changed:**\\n- `~/DEV/DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/STATUS.md`\\n- `~/.claude/global-memory/MEMORY.md`, `transcripts/.errors.log`, `transcripts/2026-09-21.md`, `transcripts/2026-09-22.md`\\n- `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json` (backup at `backup-manifest.json.bak-2026-09-22`)\\n- `~/DEV/TOS_Evidence_Brief/` (new clone), `graphify-out` (13 files from nightly job)\\n\\n**Action Items:**\\n- **Open:** memory compiler still has 2 backlogged logs; warning stays in `.errors.log` until Eric runs `compile.py` (~$1.28). Command is in `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/README.md`.\\n- Revisit the wake decision only if GPS data goes **missing**, not merely late.\\n- Carried over from earlier: test Perch/VALD failure alerts; delete `FA2026/_Mark_for_Deletion/gps_db_handoff_2026-09-21/` on Drive.\\n\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"Loading knowledge base...","durationMs":183},"type":"attachment","uuid":"57d37881-ccd4-4fef-aea6-8631e8cd55f5","timestamp":"2026-09-22T22:46:45.092Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"e907977b-fbc0-4254-8804-6e14665c3c98","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"57d37881-ccd4-4fef-aea6-8631e8cd55f5","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"a2dc1855-72b0-42c4-991f-99b2fafd717c","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\": {\"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\", \"additionalContext\": \"## Today\\nTuesday, September 22, 2026\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Knowledge Base\\n\\n286 compiled articles (214 concepts, 71 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here \\u2014 read it only when a question actually needs it:\\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \\\"<query>\\\" --top-k 5`\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Recent Daily Log\\n\\n**Context:** Ran `/dev-sync` to bring the Mac in line with GitHub, decided two open GPS items, then ran `/closeout` for 2026-09-22.\\n\\n**Key Exchanges:**\\n- Open decision on the 16:28 Mac wake for Perch/VALD pulls \\u2192 Eric chose Option 2 (leave it). He also confirmed the Windows GPS job is stopped.\\n\\n**Decisions Made:**\\n- **No `pmset` wake.** Perch and VALD may land up to 1 hour late when the Mac sleeps. Undo/enable command kept in `DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/STATUS.md`: `sudo pmset repeat wakeorpoweron MTWRFSU 16:28:00`.\\n- Windows GPS writer marked stopped (per Eric's report; not verifiable from the Mac). Two writers = two mismatched databases.\\n- Did **not** run `compile.py` \\u2014 a run costs ~$1.28, so it is Eric's call, not an auto-action.\\n\\n**Lessons Learned:**\\n- Transcript log conflicts between Mac and Windows resolve cleanly with **union merge** \\u2014 keeps every line from both machines, no `.from-*` files to clean up.\\n- A **folder name with a space** (`DEV/Linda Projects`) broke one sync step; had to redo it quoted.\\n- The backup manifest does not auto-discover new repos. `TOS_Evidence_Brief` was missing, which made the 09:05 backup report an error. Adding it to `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json` fixed it.\\n- Tier A memory does not cross machines \\u2014 durable GPS decisions were written into the repo's `docs/STATUS.md` plus `global-memory/MEMORY.md` so Windows sees them.\\n\\n**Sync results (146 repos walked):**\\n- Pulled: `_INBOX` (1), `DataBase_GPS_Reporting` (2). Pushed: `CommandCentre` (`server.out`). Cloned: `TOS_Evidence_Brief` (made on Windows 9/21). 22 active, 117 dormant and clean. No conflicts.\\n\\n**Files changed:**\\n- `~/DEV/DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/STATUS.md`\\n- `~/.claude/global-memory/MEMORY.md`, `transcripts/.errors.log`, `transcripts/2026-09-21.md`, `transcripts/2026-09-22.md`\\n- `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json` (backup at `backup-manifest.json.bak-2026-09-22`)\\n- `~/DEV/TOS_Evidence_Brief/` (new clone), `graphify-out` (13 files from nightly job)\\n\\n**Action Items:**\\n- **Open:** memory compiler still has 2 backlogged logs; warning stays in `.errors.log` until Eric runs `compile.py` (~$1.28). Command is in `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/README.md`.\\n- Revisit the wake decision only if GPS data goes **missing**, not merely late.\\n- Carried over from earlier: test Perch/VALD failure alerts; delete `FA2026/_Mark_for_Deletion/gps_db_handoff_2026-09-21/` on Drive.\\n\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"uv run python hooks/session-start.py","durationMs":182},"type":"attachment","uuid":"54bc6067-1e19-4b54-86a5-2a29e2005ed3","timestamp":"2026-09-22T22:46:45.092Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"e907977b-fbc0-4254-8804-6e14665c3c98","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"54bc6067-1e19-4b54-86a5-2a29e2005ed3","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"a2dc1855-72b0-42c4-991f-99b2fafd717c","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"SessionStart\",\"additionalContext\":\"## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\\n\\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\\n# User Profile\\n\\n## About\\nEric Rash, Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds football GPS and force-plate pipelines. Wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo on 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-first player-coach near Iowa, $90K+ floor, mid-2027. Read `~/DEV/ContextVault` before career or personal advice.\\n\\n## Preferences\\n- Python/R. macOS primary since 2026-08-05; Windows 11 still active. Tool paths: CLAUDE.md.\\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB. No cloud servers or schedulers without Baylor IT approval. Google Drive only; keep DuckDB local to avoid sync locks.\\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\\n\\n## Working Style\\n- Direct; says \\\"stop\\\" when done. Challenge vague or rehearsed reasoning: \\\"sharpened, not soothed.\\\"\\n- Decide standard technical details, state the choice, and move on.\\n- Build in small steps. Do not break mid-season work.\\n- Authors direction when leadership does not; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo group.\\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\\n\\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\\n# Working Memory\\n\\n## Active Threads\\n- **Prioritization slate (9/14)**: LIVE. 5 active, cap 5 (Fall). →Tier A prioritization-slate.\\n- **Personal site (9/2)**: NEXT: phone check; host or local? →Tier A personal-site-build-routes.\\n- **RTP CLA (9/10)**: NEXT: .ris → Zotero; lesson 0002. →Tier A rtp-cla.\\n- **ATH Tech**: shipped; ITS pending; run 9/29. →Tier A tech-inventory-refresh.\\n- **Status report**: #3 by 9/25, biweekly to 12/18. →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline.\\n- **GPS pull (9/21)**: Mac owns, 10:00+16:30. Win writer stopped (Eric, 9/22). No 16:28 wake. →`DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/`\\n- **VALD+Perch (9/21)**: Mac owns. Perch 10:05+16:35; VALD 10:10+16:40. Windows jobs off. →`_INBOX/docs/`\\n- **FB matchups (9/13)**: EPA = PFF; CFBD PPA cross-check. TRAP: `run_direction` != point of attack.\\n- **PP Recruiting (9/16)**: 010 + 011 matrix on Drive, pushed. NEXT: Eric reviews 011. →`PP_Recruiting/docs/`\\n- **Sleep scheduler (9/16)**: R1 MERGED. NEXT: night/early-kickoff itineraries. →`sleep-scheduler/docs/`\\n- **RI5 #002 (9/21)**: VERIFIED + v2 PUBLISHED to Drive (28/30 ok, 2 read OK; 7 cites + \\\"case series\\\" fixed). NEXT: Eric sends `_v2` only.\\n- **TypeSafe (9/21)**: router LIVE both boxes (Mac 149/149); verifier LIVE, reads printed pages. 7 measured off map (surgery notes last). NEXT: Eric Y/Ns 22 surgeries, 12-mo rule (PR #21). Rec: maintenance; #3 = Eric's call. →`TypeSafe_Sandbox/docs/`\\n\\n## Environment Notes\\n- Hooks: patch both copies only for shared events; Codex has no prompt-submit event. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\\n- daily-backup: manifest entries copy nothing alone; sweep nested `~/.claude` repos and flag non-main branches. →handoff 2026-09-19-backup-divergence-fix.md\\n- `DEV/Linda Projects` is a private I-983 repo. Keep private. Folder has a space; repo an underscore.\\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. →Tier A github-consolidation.\\n- Schedules →automation-cadence. CFB capture (Mon, 8/24–1/23) →cfb-forecast-capture. Portfolio dashboard →command-centre.\\n\\n## Pending Decisions\\n- **OKR gate scored 9/14; still undecided**: AI integration buy-in + data governance (Anthony's proposal). Fold into NCAA charter or parking lot?\\n- **Kitman vs MyLink**: MyLink (~half Kitman cost) + Kitman API access. Undecided; blocks priority 3.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## HOOK FAILURES — surface these to the user on the first response\\n\\n[2026-09-21T23:30:11.684558+00:00] compiler-backlog: 2 log(s) backlogged, compiling newest 2 this run - run compile.py manually to catch up\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\\n\\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (4271 chars, updated 2026-09-22 12:15)\"}}","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"Loading unified cross-harness snapshot...","durationMs":182},"type":"attachment","uuid":"bb54135b-1af1-434b-8320-3d21c3105a21","timestamp":"2026-09-22T22:46:45.092Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"e907977b-fbc0-4254-8804-6e14665c3c98","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"bb54135b-1af1-434b-8320-3d21c3105a21","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"a2dc1855-72b0-42c4-991f-99b2fafd717c","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\n  \"hookSpecificOutput\": {\n    \"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\",\n    \"additionalContext\": \"<EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\\nYou have superpowers.\\n\\n**Below is the full content of your 'superpowers:using-superpowers' skill - your introduction to using skills. For all other skills, use the 'Skill' tool:**\\n\\n---\\nname: using-superpowers\\ndescription: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\\n---\\n\\n<SUBAGENT-STOP>\\nIf you were dispatched as a subagent to execute a specific task, ignore this skill.\\n</SUBAGENT-STOP>\\n\\n<EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\\nIf you think there is even a 1% chance a skill might apply to what you are doing, you ABSOLUTELY MUST invoke the skill.\\n\\nIF A SKILL APPLIES TO YOUR TASK, YOU DO NOT HAVE A CHOICE. YOU MUST USE IT.\\n\\nThis is not negotiable. You cannot rationalize your way out of this.\\n</EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\\n\\n## The Rule\\n\\n**Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action** — including clarifying questions, exploring the codebase, or checking files. If it turns out wrong for the situation, you don't have to use it.\\n\\n**Before entering plan mode:** if you haven't already brainstormed, invoke the brainstorming skill first.\\n\\nThen announce \\\"Using [skill] to [purpose]\\\" and follow the skill exactly. If it has a checklist, create a todo per item.\\n\\n## Skill Priority\\n\\nWhen multiple skills apply, process skills come first — they set the approach, then implementation skills (frontend-design, etc.) carry it out. Brainstorming and systematic-debugging are Superpowers' most common process skills, but the rule holds for any of them.\\n\\n- \\\"Let's build X\\\" → superpowers:brainstorming first, then implementation skills.\\n- \\\"Fix this bug\\\" → superpowers:systematic-debugging first, then domain skills.\\n\\n## Red Flags\\n\\nThese thoughts mean STOP—you're rationalizing:\\n\\n| Thought | Reality |\\n|---------|---------|\\n| \\\"This is just a simple question\\\" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"I need more context first\\\" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |\\n| \\\"Let me explore the codebase first\\\" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |\\n| \\\"I can check git/files quickly\\\" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"Let me gather information first\\\" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |\\n| \\\"This doesn't need a formal skill\\\" | If a skill exists, use it. |\\n| \\\"I remember this skill\\\" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |\\n| \\\"This doesn't count as a task\\\" | Action = task. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"The skill is overkill\\\" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |\\n| \\\"I'll just do this one thing first\\\" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |\\n| \\\"This feels productive\\\" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |\\n| \\\"I know what that means\\\" | Knowing the concept ≠ using the skill. Invoke it. |\\n\\n## Platform Adaptation\\n\\nIf your harness appears here, read its reference file for special instructions:\\n\\n- Claude Code: `references/claude-code-tools.md`\\n- Codex: `references/codex-tools.md`\\n- Pi: `references/pi-tools.md`\\n- Antigravity: `references/antigravity-tools.md`\\n- Hermes Agent: `references/hermes-tools.md`\\n- Muse: `references/muse-tools.md`\\n\\n## User Instructions\\n\\nUser instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md, etc, direct requests) take precedence over skills, which in turn override default behavior. Only skip skill workflows or instructions when your human partner has explicitly told you to.\\n</EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\"\n  }\n}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"\"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/run-hook.cmd\" session-start","durationMs":244},"type":"attachment","uuid":"9f17d24a-f9b9-46a7-87e6-a1e7663612e7","timestamp":"2026-09-22T22:46:45.154Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"e907977b-fbc0-4254-8804-6e14665c3c98","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"9f17d24a-f9b9-46a7-87e6-a1e7663612e7","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"a2dc1855-72b0-42c4-991f-99b2fafd717c","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"SessionStart\",\"additionalContext\":\"\\n<context_window_protection>\\n  <priority_instructions>\\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\\n  </priority_instructions>\\n\\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \\\"timeline\\\")\\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\\n       - Each command: {label: \\\"section header\\\", command: \\\"shell command\\\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\\\"q1\\\", \\\"q2\\\", ...])\\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n\\n  <when_not_to_use>\\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\\n  </when_not_to_use>\\n\\n  <file_writing_policy>\\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\\n  </file_writing_policy>\\n\\n  <output_constraints>\\n    <artifact_policy>\\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\\n    </artifact_policy>\\n  </output_constraints>\\n  <session_continuity>\\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\\n  </session_continuity>\\n\\n  <ctx_commands>\\n    \\\"ctx stats\\\" | \\\"ctx-stats\\\" | \\\"/ctx-stats\\\" | context savings question\\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx doctor\\\" | \\\"ctx-doctor\\\" | \\\"/ctx-doctor\\\" | diagnose context-mode\\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx upgrade\\\" | \\\"ctx-upgrade\\\" | \\\"/ctx-upgrade\\\" | update context-mode\\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx purge\\\" | \\\"ctx-purge\\\" | \\\"/ctx-purge\\\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\\n\\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \\\"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\\\"\\n  </ctx_commands>\\n\\n</context_window_protection>\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/sessionstart.mjs\"","durationMs":245},"type":"attachment","uuid":"f3019d4d-63f2-450e-a1be-9335b77d39cb","timestamp":"2026-09-22T22:46:45.156Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"e907977b-fbc0-4254-8804-6e14665c3c98","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"f3019d4d-63f2-450e-a1be-9335b77d39cb","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_additional_context","content":["## Today\nTuesday, September 22, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n286 compiled articles (214 concepts, 71 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n**Context:** Ran `/dev-sync` to bring the Mac in line with GitHub, decided two open GPS items, then ran `/closeout` for 2026-09-22.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Open decision on the 16:28 Mac wake for Perch/VALD pulls → Eric chose Option 2 (leave it). He also confirmed the Windows GPS job is stopped.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- **No `pmset` wake.** Perch and VALD may land up to 1 hour late when the Mac sleeps. Undo/enable command kept in `DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/STATUS.md`: `sudo pmset repeat wakeorpoweron MTWRFSU 16:28:00`.\n- Windows GPS writer marked stopped (per Eric's report; not verifiable from the Mac). Two writers = two mismatched databases.\n- Did **not** run `compile.py` — a run costs ~$1.28, so it is Eric's call, not an auto-action.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- Transcript log conflicts between Mac and Windows resolve cleanly with **union merge** — keeps every line from both machines, no `.from-*` files to clean up.\n- A **folder name with a space** (`DEV/Linda Projects`) broke one sync step; had to redo it quoted.\n- The backup manifest does not auto-discover new repos. `TOS_Evidence_Brief` was missing, which made the 09:05 backup report an error. Adding it to `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json` fixed it.\n- Tier A memory does not cross machines — durable GPS decisions were written into the repo's `docs/STATUS.md` plus `global-memory/MEMORY.md` so Windows sees them.\n\n**Sync results (146 repos walked):**\n- Pulled: `_INBOX` (1), `DataBase_GPS_Reporting` (2). Pushed: `CommandCentre` (`server.out`). Cloned: `TOS_Evidence_Brief` (made on Windows 9/21). 22 active, 117 dormant and clean. No conflicts.\n\n**Files changed:**\n- `~/DEV/DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/STATUS.md`\n- `~/.claude/global-memory/MEMORY.md`, `transcripts/.errors.log`, `transcripts/2026-09-21.md`, `transcripts/2026-09-22.md`\n- `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json` (backup at `backup-manifest.json.bak-2026-09-22`)\n- `~/DEV/TOS_Evidence_Brief/` (new clone), `graphify-out` (13 files from nightly job)\n\n**Action Items:**\n- **Open:** memory compiler still has 2 backlogged logs; warning stays in `.errors.log` until Eric runs `compile.py` (~$1.28). Command is in `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/README.md`.\n- Revisit the wake decision only if GPS data goes **missing**, not merely late.\n- Carried over from earlier: test Perch/VALD failure alerts; delete `FA2026/_Mark_for_Deletion/gps_db_handoff_2026-09-21/` on Drive.\n","## Today\nTuesday, September 22, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n286 compiled articles (214 concepts, 71 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n**Context:** Ran `/dev-sync` to bring the Mac in line with GitHub, decided two open GPS items, then ran `/closeout` for 2026-09-22.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Open decision on the 16:28 Mac wake for Perch/VALD pulls → Eric chose Option 2 (leave it). He also confirmed the Windows GPS job is stopped.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- **No `pmset` wake.** Perch and VALD may land up to 1 hour late when the Mac sleeps. Undo/enable command kept in `DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/STATUS.md`: `sudo pmset repeat wakeorpoweron MTWRFSU 16:28:00`.\n- Windows GPS writer marked stopped (per Eric's report; not verifiable from the Mac). Two writers = two mismatched databases.\n- Did **not** run `compile.py` — a run costs ~$1.28, so it is Eric's call, not an auto-action.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- Transcript log conflicts between Mac and Windows resolve cleanly with **union merge** — keeps every line from both machines, no `.from-*` files to clean up.\n- A **folder name with a space** (`DEV/Linda Projects`) broke one sync step; had to redo it quoted.\n- The backup manifest does not auto-discover new repos. `TOS_Evidence_Brief` was missing, which made the 09:05 backup report an error. Adding it to `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json` fixed it.\n- Tier A memory does not cross machines — durable GPS decisions were written into the repo's `docs/STATUS.md` plus `global-memory/MEMORY.md` so Windows sees them.\n\n**Sync results (146 repos walked):**\n- Pulled: `_INBOX` (1), `DataBase_GPS_Reporting` (2). Pushed: `CommandCentre` (`server.out`). Cloned: `TOS_Evidence_Brief` (made on Windows 9/21). 22 active, 117 dormant and clean. No conflicts.\n\n**Files changed:**\n- `~/DEV/DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/STATUS.md`\n- `~/.claude/global-memory/MEMORY.md`, `transcripts/.errors.log`, `transcripts/2026-09-21.md`, `transcripts/2026-09-22.md`\n- `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json` (backup at `backup-manifest.json.bak-2026-09-22`)\n- `~/DEV/TOS_Evidence_Brief/` (new clone), `graphify-out` (13 files from nightly job)\n\n**Action Items:**\n- **Open:** memory compiler still has 2 backlogged logs; warning stays in `.errors.log` until Eric runs `compile.py` (~$1.28). Command is in `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/README.md`.\n- Revisit the wake decision only if GPS data goes **missing**, not merely late.\n- Carried over from earlier: test Perch/VALD failure alerts; delete `FA2026/_Mark_for_Deletion/gps_db_handoff_2026-09-21/` on Drive.\n","## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\n# User Profile\n\n## About\nEric Rash, Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds football GPS and force-plate pipelines. Wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo on 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-first player-coach near Iowa, $90K+ floor, mid-2027. Read `~/DEV/ContextVault` before career or personal advice.\n\n## Preferences\n- Python/R. macOS primary since 2026-08-05; Windows 11 still active. Tool paths: CLAUDE.md.\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB. No cloud servers or schedulers without Baylor IT approval. Google Drive only; keep DuckDB local to avoid sync locks.\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\n\n## Working Style\n- Direct; says \"stop\" when done. Challenge vague or rehearsed reasoning: \"sharpened, not soothed.\"\n- Decide standard technical details, state the choice, and move on.\n- Build in small steps. Do not break mid-season work.\n- Authors direction when leadership does not; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo group.\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\n\n---\n\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\n# Working Memory\n\n## Active Threads\n- **Prioritization slate (9/14)**: LIVE. 5 active, cap 5 (Fall). →Tier A prioritization-slate.\n- **Personal site (9/2)**: NEXT: phone check; host or local? →Tier A personal-site-build-routes.\n- **RTP CLA (9/10)**: NEXT: .ris → Zotero; lesson 0002. →Tier A rtp-cla.\n- **ATH Tech**: shipped; ITS pending; run 9/29. →Tier A tech-inventory-refresh.\n- **Status report**: #3 by 9/25, biweekly to 12/18. →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline.\n- **GPS pull (9/21)**: Mac owns, 10:00+16:30. Win writer stopped (Eric, 9/22). No 16:28 wake. →`DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/`\n- **VALD+Perch (9/21)**: Mac owns. Perch 10:05+16:35; VALD 10:10+16:40. Windows jobs off. →`_INBOX/docs/`\n- **FB matchups (9/13)**: EPA = PFF; CFBD PPA cross-check. TRAP: `run_direction` != point of attack.\n- **PP Recruiting (9/16)**: 010 + 011 matrix on Drive, pushed. NEXT: Eric reviews 011. →`PP_Recruiting/docs/`\n- **Sleep scheduler (9/16)**: R1 MERGED. NEXT: night/early-kickoff itineraries. →`sleep-scheduler/docs/`\n- **RI5 #002 (9/21)**: VERIFIED + v2 PUBLISHED to Drive (28/30 ok, 2 read OK; 7 cites + \"case series\" fixed). NEXT: Eric sends `_v2` only.\n- **TypeSafe (9/21)**: router LIVE both boxes (Mac 149/149); verifier LIVE, reads printed pages. 7 measured off map (surgery notes last). NEXT: Eric Y/Ns 22 surgeries, 12-mo rule (PR #21). Rec: maintenance; #3 = Eric's call. →`TypeSafe_Sandbox/docs/`\n\n## Environment Notes\n- Hooks: patch both copies only for shared events; Codex has no prompt-submit event. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\n- daily-backup: manifest entries copy nothing alone; sweep nested `~/.claude` repos and flag non-main branches. →handoff 2026-09-19-backup-divergence-fix.md\n- `DEV/Linda Projects` is a private I-983 repo. Keep private. Folder has a space; repo an underscore.\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. →Tier A github-consolidation.\n- Schedules →automation-cadence. CFB capture (Mon, 8/24–1/23) →cfb-forecast-capture. Portfolio dashboard →command-centre.\n\n## Pending Decisions\n- **OKR gate scored 9/14; still undecided**: AI integration buy-in + data governance (Anthony's proposal). Fold into NCAA charter or parking lot?\n- **Kitman vs MyLink**: MyLink (~half Kitman cost) + Kitman API access. Undecided; blocks priority 3.\n\n---\n\n## HOOK FAILURES — surface these to the user on the first response\n\n[2026-09-21T23:30:11.684558+00:00] compiler-backlog: 2 log(s) backlogged, compiling newest 2 this run - run compile.py manually to catch up\n\n---\n\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\n\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (4271 chars, updated 2026-09-22 12:15)","<EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\nYou have superpowers.\n\n**Below is the full content of your 'superpowers:using-superpowers' skill - your introduction to using skills. For all other skills, use the 'Skill' tool:**\n\n---\nname: using-superpowers\ndescription: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\n---\n\n<SUBAGENT-STOP>\nIf you were dispatched as a subagent to execute a specific task, ignore this skill.\n</SUBAGENT-STOP>\n\n<EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\nIf you think there is even a 1% chance a skill might apply to what you are doing, you ABSOLUTELY MUST invoke the skill.\n\nIF A SKILL APPLIES TO YOUR TASK, YOU DO NOT HAVE A CHOICE. YOU MUST USE IT.\n\nThis is not negotiable. You cannot rationalize your way out of this.\n</EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\n\n## The Rule\n\n**Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action** — including clarifying questions, exploring the codebase, or checking files. If it turns out wrong for the situation, you don't have to use it.\n\n**Before entering plan mode:** if you haven't already brainstormed, invoke the brainstorming skill first.\n\nThen announce \"Using [skill] to [purpose]\" and follow the skill exactly. If it has a checklist, create a todo per item.\n\n## Skill Priority\n\nWhen multiple skills apply, process skills come first — they set the approach, then implementation skills (frontend-design, etc.) carry it out. Brainstorming and systematic-debugging are Superpowers' most common process skills, but the rule holds for any of them.\n\n- \"Let's build X\" → superpowers:brainstorming first, then implementation skills.\n- \"Fix this bug\" → superpowers:systematic-debugging first, then domain skills.\n\n## Red Flags\n\nThese thoughts mean STOP—you're rationalizing:\n\n| Thought | Reality |\n|---------|---------|\n| \"This is just a simple question\" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |\n| \"I need more context first\" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |\n| \"Let me explore the codebase first\" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |\n| \"I can check git/files quickly\" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |\n| \"Let me gather information first\" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |\n| \"This doesn't need a formal skill\" | If a skill exists, use it. |\n| \"I remember this skill\" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |\n| \"This doesn't count as a task\" | Action = task. Check for skills. |\n| \"The skill is overkill\" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |\n| \"I'll just do this one thing first\" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |\n| \"This feels productive\" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |\n| \"I know what that means\" | Knowing the concept ≠ using the skill. Invoke it. |\n\n## Platform Adaptation\n\nIf your harness appears here, read its reference file for special instructions:\n\n- Claude Code: `references/claude-code-tools.md`\n- Codex: `references/codex-tools.md`\n- Pi: `references/pi-tools.md`\n- Antigravity: `references/antigravity-tools.md`\n- Hermes Agent: `references/hermes-tools.md`\n- Muse: `references/muse-tools.md`\n\n## User Instructions\n\nUser instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md, etc, direct requests) take precedence over skills, which in turn override default behavior. Only skip skill workflows or instructions when your human partner has explicitly told you to.\n</EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>","\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n  <ctx_commands>\n    \"ctx stats\" | \"ctx-stats\" | \"/ctx-stats\" | context savings question\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\n\n    \"ctx doctor\" | \"ctx-doctor\" | \"/ctx-doctor\" | diagnose context-mode\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx upgrade\" | \"ctx-upgrade\" | \"/ctx-upgrade\" | update context-mode\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx purge\" | \"ctx-purge\" | \"/ctx-purge\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\n\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\"\n  </ctx_commands>\n\n</context_window_protection>"],"hookName":"SessionStart","toolUseID":"SessionStart","hookEvent":"SessionStart"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"9806efd8-b99b-4fad-873e-7f5df7e64142","timestamp":"2026-09-22T22:46:45.157Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nSessionStart hook additional context: ## Today\nTuesday, September 22, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n286 compiled articles (214 concepts, 71 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n**Context:** Ran `/dev-sync` to bring the Mac in line with GitHub, decided two open GPS items, then ran `/closeout` for 2026-09-22.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Open decision on the 16:28 Mac wake for Perch/VALD pulls → Eric chose Option 2 (leave it). He also confirmed the Windows GPS job is stopped.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- **No `pmset` wake.** Perch and VALD may land up to 1 hour late when the Mac sleeps. Undo/enable command kept in `DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/STATUS.md`: `sudo pmset repeat wakeorpoweron MTWRFSU 16:28:00`.\n- Windows GPS writer marked stopped (per Eric's report; not verifiable from the Mac). Two writers = two mismatched databases.\n- Did **not** run `compile.py` — a run costs ~$1.28, so it is Eric's call, not an auto-action.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- Transcript log conflicts between Mac and Windows resolve cleanly with **union merge** — keeps every line from both machines, no `.from-*` files to clean up.\n- A **folder name with a space** (`DEV/Linda Projects`) broke one sync step; had to redo it quoted.\n- The backup manifest does not auto-discover new repos. `TOS_Evidence_Brief` was missing, which made the 09:05 backup report an error. Adding it to `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json` fixed it.\n- Tier A memory does not cross machines — durable GPS decisions were written into the repo's `docs/STATUS.md` plus `global-memory/MEMORY.md` so Windows sees them.\n\n**Sync results (146 repos walked):**\n- Pulled: `_INBOX` (1), `DataBase_GPS_Reporting` (2). Pushed: `CommandCentre` (`server.out`). Cloned: `TOS_Evidence_Brief` (made on Windows 9/21). 22 active, 117 dormant and clean. No conflicts.\n\n**Files changed:**\n- `~/DEV/DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/STATUS.md`\n- `~/.claude/global-memory/MEMORY.md`, `transcripts/.errors.log`, `transcripts/2026-09-21.md`, `transcripts/2026-09-22.md`\n- `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json` (backup at `backup-manifest.json.bak-2026-09-22`)\n- `~/DEV/TOS_Evidence_Brief/` (new clone), `graphify-out` (13 files from nightly job)\n\n**Action Items:**\n- **Open:** memory compiler still has 2 backlogged logs; warning stays in `.errors.log` until Eric runs `compile.py` (~$1.28). Command is in `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/README.md`.\n- Revisit the wake decision only if GPS data goes **missing**, not merely late.\n- Carried over from earlier: test Perch/VALD failure alerts; delete `FA2026/_Mark_for_Deletion/gps_db_handoff_2026-09-21/` on Drive.\n\n## Today\nTuesday, September 22, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n286 compiled articles (214 concepts, 71 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n**Context:** Ran `/dev-sync` to bring the Mac in line with GitHub, decided two open GPS items, then ran `/closeout` for 2026-09-22.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Open decision on the 16:28 Mac wake for Perch/VALD pulls → Eric chose Option 2 (leave it). He also confirmed the Windows GPS job is stopped.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- **No `pmset` wake.** Perch and VALD may land up to 1 hour late when the Mac sleeps. Undo/enable command kept in `DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/STATUS.md`: `sudo pmset repeat wakeorpoweron MTWRFSU 16:28:00`.\n- Windows GPS writer marked stopped (per Eric's report; not verifiable from the Mac). Two writers = two mismatched databases.\n- Did **not** run `compile.py` — a run costs ~$1.28, so it is Eric's call, not an auto-action.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- Transcript log conflicts between Mac and Windows resolve cleanly with **union merge** — keeps every line from both machines, no `.from-*` files to clean up.\n- A **folder name with a space** (`DEV/Linda Projects`) broke one sync step; had to redo it quoted.\n- The backup manifest does not auto-discover new repos. `TOS_Evidence_Brief` was missing, which made the 09:05 backup report an error. Adding it to `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json` fixed it.\n- Tier A memory does not cross machines — durable GPS decisions were written into the repo's `docs/STATUS.md` plus `global-memory/MEMORY.md` so Windows sees them.\n\n**Sync results (146 repos walked):**\n- Pulled: `_INBOX` (1), `DataBase_GPS_Reporting` (2). Pushed: `CommandCentre` (`server.out`). Cloned: `TOS_Evidence_Brief` (made on Windows 9/21). 22 active, 117 dormant and clean. No conflicts.\n\n**Files changed:**\n- `~/DEV/DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/STATUS.md`\n- `~/.claude/global-memory/MEMORY.md`, `transcripts/.errors.log`, `transcripts/2026-09-21.md`, `transcripts/2026-09-22.md`\n- `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json` (backup at `backup-manifest.json.bak-2026-09-22`)\n- `~/DEV/TOS_Evidence_Brief/` (new clone), `graphify-out` (13 files from nightly job)\n\n**Action Items:**\n- **Open:** memory compiler still has 2 backlogged logs; warning stays in `.errors.log` until Eric runs `compile.py` (~$1.28). Command is in `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/README.md`.\n- Revisit the wake decision only if GPS data goes **missing**, not merely late.\n- Carried over from earlier: test Perch/VALD failure alerts; delete `FA2026/_Mark_for_Deletion/gps_db_handoff_2026-09-21/` on Drive.\n\n## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\n# User Profile\n\n## About\nEric Rash, Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds football GPS and force-plate pipelines. Wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo on 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-first player-coach near Iowa, $90K+ floor, mid-2027. Read `~/DEV/ContextVault` before career or personal advice.\n\n## Preferences\n- Python/R. macOS primary since 2026-08-05; Windows 11 still active. Tool paths: CLAUDE.md.\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB. No cloud servers or schedulers without Baylor IT approval. Google Drive only; keep DuckDB local to avoid sync locks.\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\n\n## Working Style\n- Direct; says \"stop\" when done. Challenge vague or rehearsed reasoning: \"sharpened, not soothed.\"\n- Decide standard technical details, state the choice, and move on.\n- Build in small steps. Do not break mid-season work.\n- Authors direction when leadership does not; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo group.\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\n\n---\n\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\n# Working Memory\n\n## Active Threads\n- **Prioritization slate (9/14)**: LIVE. 5 active, cap 5 (Fall). →Tier A prioritization-slate.\n- **Personal site (9/2)**: NEXT: phone check; host or local? →Tier A personal-site-build-routes.\n- **RTP CLA (9/10)**: NEXT: .ris → Zotero; lesson 0002. →Tier A rtp-cla.\n- **ATH Tech**: shipped; ITS pending; run 9/29. →Tier A tech-inventory-refresh.\n- **Status report**: #3 by 9/25, biweekly to 12/18. →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline.\n- **GPS pull (9/21)**: Mac owns, 10:00+16:30. Win writer stopped (Eric, 9/22). No 16:28 wake. →`DataBase_GPS_Reporting/docs/`\n- **VALD+Perch (9/21)**: Mac owns. Perch 10:05+16:35; VALD 10:10+16:40. Windows jobs off. →`_INBOX/docs/`\n- **FB matchups (9/13)**: EPA = PFF; CFBD PPA cross-check. TRAP: `run_direction` != point of attack.\n- **PP Recruiting (9/16)**: 010 + 011 matrix on Drive, pushed. NEXT: Eric reviews 011. →`PP_Recruiting/docs/`\n- **Sleep scheduler (9/16)**: R1 MERGED. NEXT: night/early-kickoff itineraries. →`sleep-scheduler/docs/`\n- **RI5 #002 (9/21)**: VERIFIED + v2 PUBLISHED to Drive (28/30 ok, 2 read OK; 7 cites + \"case series\" fixed). NEXT: Eric sends `_v2` only.\n- **TypeSafe (9/21)**: router LIVE both boxes (Mac 149/149); verifier LIVE, reads printed pages. 7 measured off map (surgery notes last). NEXT: Eric Y/Ns 22 surgeries, 12-mo rule (PR #21). Rec: maintenance; #3 = Eric's call. →`TypeSafe_Sandbox/docs/`\n\n## Environment Notes\n- Hooks: patch both copies only for shared events; Codex has no prompt-submit event. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\n- daily-backup: manifest entries copy nothing alone; sweep nested `~/.claude` repos and flag non-main branches. →handoff 2026-09-19-backup-divergence-fix.md\n- `DEV/Linda Projects` is a private I-983 repo. Keep private. Folder has a space; repo an underscore.\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. →Tier A github-consolidation.\n- Schedules →automation-cadence. CFB capture (Mon, 8/24–1/23) →cfb-forecast-capture. Portfolio dashboard →command-centre.\n\n## Pending Decisions\n- **OKR gate scored 9/14; still undecided**: AI integration buy-in + data governance (Anthony's proposal). Fold into NCAA charter or parking lot?\n- **Kitman vs MyLink**: MyLink (~half Kitman cost) + Kitman API access. Undecided; blocks priority 3.\n\n---\n\n## HOOK FAILURES — surface these to the user on the first response\n\n[2026-09-21T23:30:11.684558+00:00] compiler-backlog: 2 log(s) backlogged, compiling newest 2 this run - run compile.py manually to catch up\n\n---\n\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\n\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (4271 chars, updated 2026-09-22 12:15)\n<EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\nYou have superpowers.\n\n**Below is the full content of your 'superpowers:using-superpowers' skill - your introduction to using skills. For all other skills, use the 'Skill' tool:**\n\n---\nname: using-superpowers\ndescription: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\n---\n\n<SUBAGENT-STOP>\nIf you were dispatched as a subagent to execute a specific task, ignore this skill.\n</SUBAGENT-STOP>\n\n<EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\nIf you think there is even a 1% chance a skill might apply to what you are doing, you ABSOLUTELY MUST invoke the skill.\n\nIF A SKILL APPLIES TO YOUR TASK, YOU DO NOT HAVE A CHOICE. YOU MUST USE IT.\n\nThis is not negotiable. You cannot rationalize your way out of this.\n</EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\n\n## The Rule\n\n**Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action** — including clarifying questions, exploring the codebase, or checking files. If it turns out wrong for the situation, you don't have to use it.\n\n**Before entering plan mode:** if you haven't already brainstormed, invoke the brainstorming skill first.\n\nThen announce \"Using [skill] to [purpose]\" and follow the skill exactly. If it has a checklist, create a todo per item.\n\n## Skill Priority\n\nWhen multiple skills apply, process skills come first — they set the approach, then implementation skills (frontend-design, etc.) carry it out. Brainstorming and systematic-debugging are Superpowers' most common process skills, but the rule holds for any of them.\n\n- \"Let's build X\" → superpowers:brainstorming first, then implementation skills.\n- \"Fix this bug\" → superpowers:systematic-debugging first, then domain skills.\n\n## Red Flags\n\nThese thoughts mean STOP—you're rationalizing:\n\n| Thought | Reality |\n|---------|---------|\n| \"This is just a simple question\" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |\n| \"I need more context first\" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |\n| \"Let me explore the codebase first\" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |\n| \"I can check git/files quickly\" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |\n| \"Let me gather information first\" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |\n| \"This doesn't need a formal skill\" | If a skill exists, use it. |\n| \"I remember this skill\" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |\n| \"This doesn't count as a task\" | Action = task. Check for skills. |\n| \"The skill is overkill\" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |\n| \"I'll just do this one thing first\" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |\n| \"This feels productive\" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |\n| \"I know what that means\" | Knowing the concept ≠ using the skill. Invoke it. |\n\n## Platform Adaptation\n\nIf your harness appears here, read its reference file for special instructions:\n\n- Claude Code: `references/claude-code-tools.md`\n- Codex: `references/codex-tools.md`\n- Pi: `references/pi-tools.md`\n- Antigravity: `references/antigravity-tools.md`\n- Hermes Agent: `references/hermes-tools.md`\n- Muse: `references/muse-tools.md`\n\n## User Instructions\n\nUser instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md, etc, direct requests) take precedence over skills, which in turn override default behavior. Only skip skill workflows or instructions when your human partner has explicitly told you to.\n</EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\n\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n  <ctx_commands>\n    \"ctx stats\" | \"ctx-stats\" | \"/ctx-stats\" | context savings question\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\n\n    \"ctx doctor\" | \"ctx-doctor\" | \"/ctx-doctor\" | diagnose context-mode\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx upgrade\" | \"ctx-upgrade\" | \"/ctx-upgrade\" | update context-mode\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx purge\" | \"ctx-purge\" | \"/ctx-purge\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\n\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\"\n  </ctx_commands>\n\n</context_window_protection>\n</system-reminder>"}],"userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"e907977b-fbc0-4254-8804-6e14665c3c98","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"9806efd8-b99b-4fad-873e-7f5df7e64142","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"d971ae3b-f016-4baf-888c-67abacdccdab","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"Review the conversation context below and respond with a concise summary\nof important items that should be preserved in the daily log.\nDo NOT use any tools — just return plain text.\n\nFormat your response as a structured daily log entry with these sections:\n\n**Context:** [One line about what the user was working on]\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- [Important Q&A or discussions]\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- [Any decisions with rationale]\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- [Gotchas, patterns, or insights discovered]\n\n**Action Items:**\n- [Follow-ups or TODOs mentioned]\n\nSkip anything that is:\n- Routine tool calls or file reads\n- Content that's trivial or obvious\n- Trivial back-and-forth or clarification exchanges\n\nOnly include sections that have actual content. If nothing is worth saving,\nrespond with exactly: FLUSH_OK\n\n## Conversation Context\n\n**Assistant:** Still checking. I scanned your install, plugins, and 50 recent sessions. Now I check skill files, hooks, and one hook that shows many errors.\n\n**Assistant:** Here is the report.\n\n**Short version:** Your setup is healthy. Claude Code is current, and auto mode is already your default. I found 3 small cleanups. Every change can be undone.\n\n## What is fine\n\n- **Install:** One copy only, the native install at `~/.local/bin/claude`. No leftover npm copy.\n- **Version:** 2.1.280. That is the latest version.\n- **Settings files:** All of them open with no errors.\n- **Auto mode:** It is already your default in `~/.claude/settings.json`. I change nothing.\n- **Agent files:** You have none, so nothing can break there.\n- **CLAUDE.md files:** They are small, about 1.9k est. tokens in total. `~/DEV/CLAUDE.md` is a map, and nothing in it can be derived from the code. There are no duplicates and no conflicts.\n- **Permissions:** 38 actions were blocked. None of them was a simple read-only command, so I have no allow rules to suggest.\n\n## What I would clean up\n\n| Component | Type | Uses (total since install) | Used in last 30 days? | Est. resident tokens | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| desktop-commander@synced | plugin | 0 | No | ~700 | Remove |\n| firecrawl@synced | plugin | 0 | No | ~300 | Remove |\n| browser-use@synced | plugin (add-on bundle) + MCP server (a link to an outside tool) | 0 | No | ~500 | Remove |\n| exa@synced | plugin | 0 | No | ~150 | Remove |\n| cowork-plugin-management@synced | plugin | 0 | No | ~250 | Remove. It also only works in Cowork. |\n| HyperFrames by HeyGen | claude.ai connector | n/a (no counter) | No | ~400 (its instructions load in every session) | Remove. It also blocks its main tools in Claude Code. |\n| persistent-localhost | skill | used before | No | name-only | Fix it. Do not remove it. |\n| Claude Docs | claude.ai connector | n/a (no counter) | No | deferred | Keep. It is new, so there is not enough data yet. |\n| Everything else | — | — | Yes | — | Keep. It gets real use. |\n\nThe main check covered 50 sessions over 3.4 days. For the plugins, MCP servers and skills, I also checked 303 sessions over 30 days.\n\n## Proposed actions\n\n1. **Turn off the 5 unused plugins.** I add these 5 keys, set to `false`, under `enabledPlugins` in `~/.claude/settings.json`:\n   - `desktop-commander@synced`\n   - `firecrawl@synced`\n   - `browser-use@synced`\n   - `exa@synced`\n   - `cowork-plugin-management@synced`\n\n   This saves about 1.9k est. tokens in every session.\n2. **Fix a broken skill header.** The file is `~/.agents/skills/persistent-localhost/SKILL.md`.\n   - The problem: the `description:` line contains a `: \"` sequence, and that breaks the header. So Claude Code ignores the whole header.\n   - The fix: I change it to `description: >-` and put the same text on the lines below. The words stay the same.\n   - Note: this is a vendored skill (a copy from someone else). A vendor update can put the broken version back.\n3. **HyperFrames: you do this step yourself.** Type `/mcp`, select HyperFrames, and disable it. This only applies to the project you are in when you run it.\n\n## Warnings only (I will not change these)\n\n- **The dcg hook fails on Windows.** It failed 913 times in Windows sessions. The Windows settings point to `/Users/kristinarash/.local/bin/dcg`, which is the Mac path, so dcg is not found there. The result: the dcg guard does not check anything on Windows. Fix it on the Windows laptop. Your memory note \"Config encoding damage from Windows\" is related.\n- **The skill-router hook is slow.** It runs on every message you send. It usually takes 1.6 s. The worst run took 68 s, and it hit its timeout 2 times.\n- For an exact live count of what uses context, run `/context`. My numbers are estimates.\n\n**User:** [Request interrupted by user for tool use]"},"uuid":"dc7d0cec-8b97-4275-9b63-b2b06c069619","timestamp":"2026-09-22T22:46:46.645Z","permissionMode":"auto","promptSource":"sdk","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"e907977b-fbc0-4254-8804-6e14665c3c98","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
